                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "we need to understand the definitions of the properties listed. \n\nFrequency: The number of cycles of a wave that occur in one second, measured in Hertz (Hz). \n\nAmplitude: The maximum displacement of a wave from its equilibrium position, measured in decibels (dB). \n\nWavelength: The distance between two consecutive points on a wave that are in phase, measured in meters (m). \n\nSpeed in air: The speed at which sound waves travel through air, which is approximately 343 meters per second (m/s) at room temperature. \n\nSince both sound waves have the same pitch, their frequency and wavelength must be the same. Therefore, we can eliminate options A and C. \n\nThe second sound wave is louder, which means it has a greater amplitude. Therefore, the correct answer is B) Amplitude. \n\nThe speed of sound in air is a constant value and does not change based on the amplitude or pitch of the sound wave. Therefore, we can eliminate option D.",
                        "answers": [
