                        "cot": "\n\nSince both sound waves have the same pitch, we can eliminate frequency and wavelength as possible answers. The pitch of a sound wave is determined by its frequency, and the distance between two consecutive peaks or troughs is the wavelength. Therefore, if the pitch is the same, the frequency and wavelength must also be the same for both sound waves.\n\nThe only property that can explain why the second sound wave is louder is amplitude. Amplitude is the maximum displacement of a particle from its rest position as a wave passes through it. In other words, it measures the strength or intensity of the wave. The greater the amplitude, the louder the sound.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Amplitude.",
