                    {
                        "id": "2520783d-4942-4f7b-bfcb-70bd07be2983",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Sound waves are longitudinal waves that travel through a medium, such as air. They have properties such as frequency, amplitude, wavelength, and speed.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is frequency?\n2. What is amplitude?\n3. What is wavelength?\n4. What is the relationship between frequency, amplitude, and wavelength?\n5. Can the speed of sound waves change in air?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Frequency is the number of cycles of a wave that occur in one second, measured in Hertz (Hz).\n2. Amplitude is the maximum displacement of a wave from its equilibrium position, measured in decibels (dB).\n3. Wavelength is the distance between two consecutive points on a wave that are in phase, measured in meters (m).\n4. Frequency, amplitude, and wavelength are related by the equation v = f\u03bb, where v is the speed of the wave, f is the frequency, and \u03bb is the wavelength. This means that if the frequency increases, the wavelength decreases, and vice versa. If the amplitude increases, the wave becomes louder.\n5. The speed of sound waves in air is approximately 343 meters per second at room temperature and normal atmospheric pressure. This speed can change depending on the temperature, humidity, and pressure of the air.\n\nAnswer: B) Amplitude is greater for the second sound wave.",
                        "answers": [
