                    {
                        "id": "8d5151fb-e540-4708-bafb-16eb1b0915d0",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I have a question about sound waves. If a person produces two sound waves with a flute, one immediately after the other, and both sound waves have the same pitch, but the second one is louder, which of the following properties is greater for the second sound wave?\n\nProfessor: That's a great question. The property that is greater for the second sound wave is the amplitude.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what amplitude is?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Amplitude is the maximum displacement of a wave from its equilibrium position. In simpler terms, it's the height of the wave. The greater the amplitude, the louder the sound.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand that. But what about the other properties listed in the question?\n\nProfessor: The frequency is the number of waves that pass a fixed point in a given amount of time. Since both sound waves have the same pitch, they have the same frequency. The wavelength is the distance between two consecutive peaks or troughs of a wave. The speed in air is the speed at which the sound wave travels through the air. These properties are not affected by the loudness of the sound wave.\n\nStudent: I see. So, just to clarify, the amplitude is the property that determines the loudness of the sound wave?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. The greater the amplitude, the louder the sound.",
                        "answers": [
