                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The parts of a plant that capture sunlight and use it to make food are called chloroplasts. Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll, which is the pigment that absorbs sunlight. \n\nTo answer the question, we need to think about where chloroplasts are most likely to be found in a plant. \n\nA) Roots: Roots are responsible for absorbing water and nutrients from the soil. They do not need to capture sunlight, so it is unlikely that you would find many chloroplasts in the roots. \n\nB) Stems: Stems provide support for the plant and transport water and nutrients between the roots and leaves. While some stems may have small amounts of chloroplasts, they are not the main site of photosynthesis in the plant. \n\nC) Leaves: Leaves are the primary site of photosynthesis in a plant. They are flat and thin, which allows for maximum exposure to sunlight. Chloroplasts are abundant in the cells of leaves, especially in the mesophyll layer. \n\nD) Flowers: Flowers are reproductive structures of the plant and do not play a role in photosynthesis. It is unlikely that you would find many chloroplasts in the flowers. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) leaves.",
