                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understanding learned behavior\nLearned behavior refers to actions or responses that an animal develops through experience or observation. These behaviors are not innate or instinctual but are acquired through practice or training.\n\nStep 2: Analyzing the options\nA) Making a den in the hollow of a tree: Raccoons are known to make dens in the hollow of trees, and this behavior is likely to be instinctual rather than learned. Raccoons are born with the ability to climb trees and use their sharp claws to dig into the bark and create a den.\n\nB) Opening a trashcan looking for food: Raccoons are opportunistic feeders and are known to scavenge for food in urban areas. While this behavior may be influenced by their natural instincts to forage for food, it is also likely to be learned through experience. Raccoons may observe other raccoons or learn from trial and error that trash cans are a good source of food.\n\nC) Moving four legs while running: This behavior is innate and instinctual for raccoons. Like all mammals, raccoons are born with the ability to move their four legs while running.\n\nD) Having a litter of four babies: This behavior is also innate and instinctual for raccoons. Female raccoons give birth to litters of 2-5 babies, and this behavior is not learned but rather a natural part of their reproductive cycle.\n\nStep 3: Conclusion\nBased on the analysis, option B) opening a trashcan looking for food is most likely a learned behavior for a raccoon. While raccoons are naturally opportunistic feeders, their ability to open trash cans and other containers to access food is likely to be learned through experience and observation.",
