                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Raccoons are intelligent and adaptable animals that are found throughout North America. They are known for their distinctive black mask and ringed tail. Raccoons are omnivores and will eat a variety of foods, including fruits, nuts, insects, and small animals. They are also known for their ability to use their front paws like hands to manipulate objects.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is a learned behavior?\n2. Can raccoons learn behaviors?\n3. What behaviors are common for raccoons?\n4. Which of the given options are behaviors that raccoons can learn?\n\nAnswer:\n1. A learned behavior is a behavior that an animal develops through experience or observation.\n2. Yes, raccoons are capable of learning behaviors.\n3. Common behaviors for raccoons include climbing trees, swimming, and foraging for food.\n4. Option B, opening a trashcan looking for food, is most likely a learned behavior for a raccoon. While raccoons are natural foragers, they typically hunt for food on the ground or in trees. However, as humans have encroached on their habitats, raccoons have learned to scavenge for food in urban areas, including trash cans. Making a den in the hollow of a tree is a natural behavior for raccoons, as is moving four legs while running. Having a litter of four babies is also a natural behavior for raccoons.",
