                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is D) genetic variation.\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- Genetic mutation (option A) can introduce new traits into a population, but it does not necessarily lead to an increased survival rate. Mutations can be harmful, neutral, or beneficial, and their effects on survival depend on the environment and the specific trait involved. Moreover, mutations occur randomly and are not necessarily more common in individuals with higher survival rates.\n- Species competition (option B) can indeed affect the survival of individuals, but it does not explain why some organisms within the same species have a higher survival rate than others. Competition occurs between different species that share the same resources, not within a single species.\n- Asexual reproduction (option C) means that offspring are genetically identical to the parent, which reduces genetic variation within a population. Therefore, asexual reproduction does not allow for some organisms to have an increased survival rate over others based on genetic differences.\n- Genetic variation (option D) refers to the diversity of traits within a population due to differences in genes and alleles. This variation can arise from mutations, sexual reproduction, gene flow, and other factors. Genetic variation can provide some individuals with traits that are better suited to the environment, such as resistance to diseases, tolerance to extreme temperatures, or better camouflage. Therefore, individuals with these advantageous traits are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing on their genes to the next generation. Over time, this can lead to the evolution of new species or subspecies.",
