                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: In any population, there is variation in traits among individuals. Some of these traits may be advantageous for survival and reproduction, while others may be detrimental. Over time, the individuals with advantageous traits are more likely to survive and pass on their genes to the next generation, leading to a higher frequency of those traits in the population.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is genetic mutation?\n2. How does species competition affect survival rates?\n3. What is asexual reproduction?\n4. What is genetic variation?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Genetic mutation is a change in the DNA sequence that can result in a new trait. Mutations can be beneficial, harmful, or neutral.\n2. Species competition occurs when individuals of the same or different species compete for limited resources such as food, water, and shelter. This can lead to some individuals having a higher chance of survival if they are better adapted to the environment or have a competitive advantage.\n3. Asexual reproduction is a type of reproduction that does not involve the fusion of gametes (sex cells). Offspring are produced from a single parent and are genetically identical to the parent.\n4. Genetic variation refers to the differences in DNA sequences among individuals of the same species. This variation can be caused by genetic mutations, sexual reproduction, or other factors. Genetic variation can lead to differences in traits among individuals, some of which may be advantageous for survival and reproduction.\n\nAnswer: D) Genetic variation allows for some organisms in a population to have an increased survival rate over other organisms of the same species. This is because genetic variation can lead to differences in traits among individuals, some of which may be advantageous for survival and reproduction. Over time, the individuals with advantageous traits are more likely to survive and pass on their genes to the next generation, leading to a higher frequency of those traits in the population.",
