                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Write out the genotypes of the parent guinea pigs. Both are Bb, meaning they each have one dominant black allele (B) and one recessive white allele (b).\n\nStep 2: Use a Punnett square to determine the possible genotypes of their offspring. \n\n|   | B | b |\n|---|---|---|\n| B | BB | Bb |\n| b | Bb | bb |\n\nStep 3: Determine the phenotypes (physical appearance) of the offspring based on their genotypes. \n\n- BB: black\n- Bb: black\n- bb: white\n\nStep 4: Calculate the ratio of possible phenotypes. \n\n- 2/4 (or 1/2) of the offspring will have the genotype Bb, which will result in black fur. \n- 1/4 of the offspring will have the genotype BB, which will also result in black fur. \n- 1/4 of the offspring will have the genotype bb, which will result in white fur. \n\nTherefore, the answer is C) 50% black, 50% white.",
