                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the atomic number of the element, which is equal to the number of protons in the nucleus.\n2. Identify the mass number of the element, which is equal to the sum of the number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus.\n3. Subtract the atomic number from the mass number to determine the number of neutrons in the nucleus.\n4. Use the periodic table to determine the atomic mass of the element, which is the weighted average of the masses of all the isotopes of the element.\n5. Round the atomic mass to the nearest whole number to obtain the mass of the atom in atomic mass units (amu).\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. The atomic number is the number of protons in the nucleus, so we need to add the number of protons to something to get the mass of the atom. Eliminate options A and D.\n2. The mass number is the sum of the number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus, so we need to add the number of protons to the number of neutrons to get the mass of the atom. Option B matches this step, so it could be the correct answer.\n3. Option C suggests subtracting the number of protons from the number of neutrons, which is not correct according to step 2. Eliminate option C.\n4. The periodic table provides the atomic mass of the element, which is the weighted average of the masses of all the isotopes of the element. This step is not mentioned in any of the options, but it is necessary to obtain the correct answer.\n5. Once we have the atomic mass, we round it to the nearest whole number to obtain the mass of the atom in amu. This step is not mentioned in any of the options, but it is necessary to obtain the correct answer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) add the number of protons to the number of neutrons. However, it is important to note that additional steps are necessary to obtain the correct answer.",
