                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: When two objects of different temperatures come into contact, heat energy will transfer from the warmer object to the cooler object until they reach the same temperature.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the temperature of the ice cube?\n2. What is the temperature of the warm room?\n3. What happens when the ice cube and the warm room come into contact?\n4. How does heat energy transfer between the ice cube and the warm room?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The temperature of the ice cube is below freezing, typically around 0\u00b0C or 32\u00b0F.\n2. The temperature of the warm room is above freezing, typically around 20-25\u00b0C or 68-77\u00b0F.\n3. When the ice cube and the warm room come into contact, heat energy will transfer from the warmer room to the cooler ice cube.\n4. Heat energy transfers from the warm room to the ice cube through a process called conduction. The molecules in the warm air collide with the molecules on the surface of the ice cube, transferring their energy to the ice cube. As the ice cube absorbs more heat energy, its molecules begin to vibrate faster and move farther apart, causing the ice to melt and turn into liquid water. The heat energy from the ice cube then moves into the air through a process called convection, where the warmer air rises and cooler air sinks. Therefore, the correct answer is A) Heat from the room is absorbed by the ice cube.",
