                    {
                        "id": "69a3be60-95e3-4bd5-bfdb-0bc8e0592d52",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain how people used their knowledge of the stars centuries ago?\n\nProfessor: Of course. People used the stars to navigate ships across the oceans.\n\nStudent: How did they do that?\n\nProfessor: They used the position of the stars in the sky to determine their location and direction. By comparing the position of the stars to a chart, sailors could figure out where they were and where they needed to go.\n\nStudent: Did they use any specific stars for navigation?\n\nProfessor: Yes, they used a group of stars called the North Star or Polaris. This star is always in the same position in the sky, so sailors could use it to determine their direction.\n\nStudent: What about studying the weather? How did they use the stars for that?\n\nProfessor: Well, they didn't use the stars directly to study the weather. But they did use the stars to predict the changing of the seasons, which could give them an idea of what kind of weather to expect.\n\nStudent: And what about predicting volcanic eruptions?\n\nProfessor: I don't think people used the stars to predict volcanic eruptions. They probably used other methods, like monitoring the behavior of the volcano itself.\n\nStudent: Okay, and finally, did people use the stars to map the Earth's surface?\n\nProfessor: Yes, they did. By observing the position of the stars from different locations on Earth, people were able to create more accurate maps of the planet. This technique is called triangulation.",
