                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to understand what the new satellite technology is designed to do. The passage tells us that it is being developed to help predict the weather. This means that it will be able to gather data about atmospheric conditions and use that information to make more accurate forecasts about what the weather will be like in the future.\n\nNext, we need to consider how this technology will benefit people. Option A, \"It will show people that storms can be prevented,\" is not a likely outcome of this technology. While it may be possible to take some preventative measures based on weather forecasts, such as evacuating an area before a hurricane hits, it is not realistic to think that we can prevent storms from occurring altogether.\n\nOption B, \"It will show people that science is interesting,\" is also not a likely outcome. While some people may find the technology itself interesting, the main goal of developing it is to improve weather forecasting, not to generate public interest in science.\n\nOption D, \"It will help people drive in the rain,\" is a possible benefit of improved weather forecasting, but it is not the most significant one. While more accurate forecasts could help people plan their travel routes and avoid dangerous driving conditions, the potential impact of this benefit is relatively small compared to the other options.\n\nThis leaves us with option C, \"It will help warn people about dangerous storms,\" as the most likely answer. Improved weather forecasting can help people prepare for and respond to severe weather events, such as hurricanes, tornadoes, and floods. By providing more accurate and timely warnings, this technology can help save lives and reduce the damage caused by these storms.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) It will help warn people about dangerous storms.",
